{
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "gatelint",
          "version": "dev",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "GL001",
              "name": "ScannerFailureSwallowedByTheShell",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner failure swallowed by the shell"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scanner's exit status is discarded by the shell, so a failing scan can never fail the job. The usual culprit is \"|| true\", but \"|| :\", \"|| exit 0\", \"|| echo ...\", a leading \"!\", running the scanner inside $(...) as an argument of another command, running it in the background with \"&\", using it as an if condition without a failing branch, and putting it on the left of \"&&\" in the middle of a script (bash exempts that command from errexit) all have the same effect. The scanner still runs and prints findings, so the job looks protected while it is not. gatelint only reports this when the swallowed command is a known scanner."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scanner's exit status is discarded by the shell, so a failing scan can never fail the job. The usual culprit is \"|| true\", but \"|| :\", \"|| exit 0\", \"|| echo ...\", a leading \"!\", running the scanner inside $(...) as an argument of another command, running it in the background with \"&\", using it as an if condition without a failing branch, and putting it on the left of \"&&\" in the middle of a script (bash exempts that command from errexit) all have the same effect. The scanner still runs and prints findings, so the job looks protected while it is not. gatelint only reports this when the swallowed command is a known scanner.\n\nFix: remove the \"|| true\" (or equivalent) so a failing scan fails the job",
                "markdown": "The scanner's exit status is discarded by the shell, so a failing scan can never fail the job. The usual culprit is \"|| true\", but \"|| :\", \"|| exit 0\", \"|| echo ...\", a leading \"!\", running the scanner inside $(...) as an argument of another command, running it in the background with \"&\", using it as an if condition without a failing branch, and putting it on the left of \"&&\" in the middle of a script (bash exempts that command from errexit) all have the same effect. The scanner still runs and prints findings, so the job looks protected while it is not. gatelint only reports this when the swallowed command is a known scanner.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan image\n  run: trivy image --exit-code 1 myapp:latest || true\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan image\n  run: trivy image --exit-code 1 myapp:latest\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL001.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL002",
              "name": "ErrexitDisabledBeforeAScanner",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "errexit disabled before a scanner"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\"set +e\" (or a custom shell started without -e, such as \"shell: bash {0}\") turns off errexit, so the script keeps going after a command fails. When a scanner runs while errexit is off and it is not the last command of the script, its failure is lost: the step's exit status is that of whatever runs last. gatelint does not report the pattern when the next command reads $? (for example \"rc=$?\") or when the scanner is the final command, because then the status still reaches the runner."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "\"set +e\" (or a custom shell started without -e, such as \"shell: bash {0}\") turns off errexit, so the script keeps going after a command fails. When a scanner runs while errexit is off and it is not the last command of the script, its failure is lost: the step's exit status is that of whatever runs last. gatelint does not report the pattern when the next command reads $? (for example \"rc=$?\") or when the scanner is the final command, because then the status still reaches the runner.\n\nFix: remove \"set +e\", or capture the status with rc=$? and exit with it",
                "markdown": "\"set +e\" (or a custom shell started without -e, such as \"shell: bash {0}\") turns off errexit, so the script keeps going after a command fails. When a scanner runs while errexit is off and it is not the last command of the script, its failure is lost: the step's exit status is that of whatever runs last. gatelint does not report the pattern when the next command reads $? (for example \"rc=$?\") or when the scanner is the final command, because then the status still reaches the runner.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan\n  run: |\n    set +e\n    gitleaks detect --source .\n    echo \"scan finished\"\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan\n  run: |\n    gitleaks detect --source .\n    echo \"scan finished\"\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL002.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL003",
              "name": "ScannerPipedWithoutPipefail",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner piped without pipefail"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Without pipefail, a pipeline's exit status is that of its last command. \"trivy image app | tee report.txt\" therefore succeeds whenever tee succeeds, whatever Trivy found. On GitHub Actions the default shell for Linux and macOS runners when \"shell:\" is omitted is \"bash -e {0}\", which does not set pipefail; \"shell: bash\" runs \"bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}\" and does. \"shell: sh\" runs \"sh -e {0}\" without pipefail. GitLab Runner enables pipefail where the shell supports it, so on GitLab this rule only fires after an explicit \"set +o pipefail\"."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Without pipefail, a pipeline's exit status is that of its last command. \"trivy image app | tee report.txt\" therefore succeeds whenever tee succeeds, whatever Trivy found. On GitHub Actions the default shell for Linux and macOS runners when \"shell:\" is omitted is \"bash -e {0}\", which does not set pipefail; \"shell: bash\" runs \"bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}\" and does. \"shell: sh\" runs \"sh -e {0}\" without pipefail. GitLab Runner enables pipefail where the shell supports it, so on GitLab this rule only fires after an explicit \"set +o pipefail\".\n\nFix: set \"shell: bash\" on the step (it adds -o pipefail) or add \"set -o pipefail\" before the pipeline",
                "markdown": "Without pipefail, a pipeline's exit status is that of its last command. \"trivy image app | tee report.txt\" therefore succeeds whenever tee succeeds, whatever Trivy found. On GitHub Actions the default shell for Linux and macOS runners when \"shell:\" is omitted is \"bash -e {0}\", which does not set pipefail; \"shell: bash\" runs \"bash --noprofile --norc -eo pipefail {0}\" and does. \"shell: sh\" runs \"sh -e {0}\" without pipefail. GitLab Runner enables pipefail where the shell supports it, so on GitLab this rule only fires after an explicit \"set +o pipefail\".\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan\n  run: trivy fs --exit-code 1 . | tee trivy.txt\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Scan\n  shell: bash\n  run: trivy fs --exit-code 1 . | tee trivy.txt\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL003.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL004",
              "name": "ContinueOnErrorOnAScannerStepOrJob",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "continue-on-error on a scanner step or job"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\"continue-on-error: true\" tells GitHub Actions to treat a failed step (or job) as a success. On a scanner step this keeps the workflow green whatever the scanner finds; the only trace is a warning annotation that is easy to miss. When the value is an expression such as \"${{ matrix.experimental }}\", gatelint reports it at medium severity as conditional, because the gate is only disabled for some runs."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "\"continue-on-error: true\" tells GitHub Actions to treat a failed step (or job) as a success. On a scanner step this keeps the workflow green whatever the scanner finds; the only trace is a warning annotation that is easy to miss. When the value is an expression such as \"${{ matrix.experimental }}\", gatelint reports it at medium severity as conditional, because the gate is only disabled for some runs.\n\nFix: remove continue-on-error, or move non-blocking experiments to a separate job",
                "markdown": "\"continue-on-error: true\" tells GitHub Actions to treat a failed step (or job) as a success. On a scanner step this keeps the workflow green whatever the scanner finds; the only trace is a warning annotation that is easy to miss. When the value is an expression such as \"${{ matrix.experimental }}\", gatelint reports it at medium severity as conditional, because the gate is only disabled for some runs.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Semgrep\n  run: semgrep ci\n  continue-on-error: true\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Semgrep\n  run: semgrep ci\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL004.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL005",
              "name": "ScannerJobAllowedToFail",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner job allowed to fail"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "\"allow_failure: true\" lets a GitLab job fail without failing the pipeline; the merge request shows a warning icon and can still be merged. \"allow_failure: exit_codes\" does the same for specific exit codes, and when those include the scanner's failure code (1 for most tools, 2 for Grype with --fail-on, 183 for TruffleHog, or the value given to --exit-code) the scan can never block the pipeline. The same applies to an allow_failure inside rules: when it covers every rule that runs the job (reported as conditional when only some rules set it), and to scanners placed in after_script, whose exit status GitLab ignores."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "\"allow_failure: true\" lets a GitLab job fail without failing the pipeline; the merge request shows a warning icon and can still be merged. \"allow_failure: exit_codes\" does the same for specific exit codes, and when those include the scanner's failure code (1 for most tools, 2 for Grype with --fail-on, 183 for TruffleHog, or the value given to --exit-code) the scan can never block the pipeline. The same applies to an allow_failure inside rules: when it covers every rule that runs the job (reported as conditional when only some rules set it), and to scanners placed in after_script, whose exit status GitLab ignores.\n\nFix: remove allow_failure from the scanner job (or drop the scanner's failure code from exit_codes)",
                "markdown": "\"allow_failure: true\" lets a GitLab job fail without failing the pipeline; the merge request shows a warning icon and can still be merged. \"allow_failure: exit_codes\" does the same for specific exit codes, and when those include the scanner's failure code (1 for most tools, 2 for Grype with --fail-on, 183 for TruffleHog, or the value given to --exit-code) the scan can never block the pipeline. The same applies to an allow_failure inside rules: when it covers every rule that runs the job (reported as conditional when only some rules set it), and to scanners placed in after_script, whose exit status GitLab ignores.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\ntrivy:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - trivy fs --exit-code 1 .\n  allow_failure: true\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\ntrivy:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - trivy fs --exit-code 1 .\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL005.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL006",
              "name": "NeuteringFlagOnTheScannerCommandLine",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Neutering flag on the scanner command line"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Many scanners have a flag that keeps the exit code at 0 whatever they find: Bandit's --exit-zero, Checkov's and tfsec's --soft-fail, gosec's -no-fail, \"--exit-code 0\" for Trivy and Gitleaks, \"npm audit --audit-level=none\". The same applies to the equivalent environment variables, such as TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=0. The flag is often added \"temporarily\" to get a pipeline green and never removed. gatelint knows each scanner's flags from its catalog, which can be extended in .gatelint.yml."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Many scanners have a flag that keeps the exit code at 0 whatever they find: Bandit's --exit-zero, Checkov's and tfsec's --soft-fail, gosec's -no-fail, \"--exit-code 0\" for Trivy and Gitleaks, \"npm audit --audit-level=none\". The same applies to the equivalent environment variables, such as TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=0. The flag is often added \"temporarily\" to get a pipeline green and never removed. gatelint knows each scanner's flags from its catalog, which can be extended in .gatelint.yml.\n\nFix: remove the flag so the scanner exits non-zero on findings",
                "markdown": "Many scanners have a flag that keeps the exit code at 0 whatever they find: Bandit's --exit-zero, Checkov's and tfsec's --soft-fail, gosec's -no-fail, \"--exit-code 0\" for Trivy and Gitleaks, \"npm audit --audit-level=none\". The same applies to the equivalent environment variables, such as TRIVY_EXIT_CODE=0. The flag is often added \"temporarily\" to get a pipeline green and never removed. gatelint knows each scanner's flags from its catalog, which can be extended in .gatelint.yml.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\nscript:\n  - bandit -r src --exit-zero\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\nscript:\n  - bandit -r src\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL006.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL007",
              "name": "ScannerDoesNotGateByDefault",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner does not gate by default"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Some scanners report findings but exit 0 unless asked to fail: Trivy needs --exit-code 1, Grype needs --fail-on, \"semgrep scan\" needs --error, TruffleHog needs --fail, and aquasecurity/trivy-action needs exit-code set to a non-zero value. Without the gating option the scan is informational only, which is easy to miss because the job log is full of findings. \"snyk monitor\" never fails on vulnerabilities and is reported at info severity; use \"snyk test\" to gate. When the gating option comes from a configuration file or an expression gatelint cannot evaluate, the scanner is listed as \"unknown\" in the coverage summary instead."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Some scanners report findings but exit 0 unless asked to fail: Trivy needs --exit-code 1, Grype needs --fail-on, \"semgrep scan\" needs --error, TruffleHog needs --fail, and aquasecurity/trivy-action needs exit-code set to a non-zero value. Without the gating option the scan is informational only, which is easy to miss because the job log is full of findings. \"snyk monitor\" never fails on vulnerabilities and is reported at info severity; use \"snyk test\" to gate. When the gating option comes from a configuration file or an expression gatelint cannot evaluate, the scanner is listed as \"unknown\" in the coverage summary instead.\n\nFix: add the scanner's gating option so findings fail the build",
                "markdown": "Some scanners report findings but exit 0 unless asked to fail: Trivy needs --exit-code 1, Grype needs --fail-on, \"semgrep scan\" needs --error, TruffleHog needs --fail, and aquasecurity/trivy-action needs exit-code set to a non-zero value. Without the gating option the scan is informational only, which is easy to miss because the job log is full of findings. \"snyk monitor\" never fails on vulnerabilities and is reported at info severity; use \"snyk test\" to gate. When the gating option comes from a configuration file or an expression gatelint cannot evaluate, the scanner is listed as \"unknown\" in the coverage summary instead.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Trivy\n  uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.28.0\n  with:\n    scan-type: fs\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- name: Trivy\n  uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@0.28.0\n  with:\n    scan-type: fs\n    exit-code: '1'\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL007.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "5.5"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL008",
              "name": "NeuteringInputOnAScannerAction",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Neutering input on a scanner action"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner actions expose inputs that keep the step green whatever is found: aquasecurity/trivy-action with exit-code: '0', anchore/scan-action with fail-build: false, bridgecrewio/checkov-action and aquasecurity/tfsec-action with soft_fail: true, pypa/gh-action-pip-audit with internal-be-careful-allow-failure: true, and neutering flags passed through an args input (for example securego/gosec with args: -no-fail). These inputs are usually added to unblock a release and then forgotten."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Scanner actions expose inputs that keep the step green whatever is found: aquasecurity/trivy-action with exit-code: '0', anchore/scan-action with fail-build: false, bridgecrewio/checkov-action and aquasecurity/tfsec-action with soft_fail: true, pypa/gh-action-pip-audit with internal-be-careful-allow-failure: true, and neutering flags passed through an args input (for example securego/gosec with args: -no-fail). These inputs are usually added to unblock a release and then forgotten.\n\nFix: remove the input (or set it to its gating value) so findings fail the step",
                "markdown": "Scanner actions expose inputs that keep the step green whatever is found: aquasecurity/trivy-action with exit-code: '0', anchore/scan-action with fail-build: false, bridgecrewio/checkov-action and aquasecurity/tfsec-action with soft_fail: true, pypa/gh-action-pip-audit with internal-be-careful-allow-failure: true, and neutering flags passed through an args input (for example securego/gosec with args: -no-fail). These inputs are usually added to unblock a release and then forgotten.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n- uses: anchore/scan-action@v6\n  with:\n    image: myapp:latest\n    fail-build: false\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n- uses: anchore/scan-action@v6\n  with:\n    image: myapp:latest\n    severity-cutoff: high\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL008.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL009",
              "name": "ScannerDisabled",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Scanner disabled"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The scanner is configured but never runs automatically, so it cannot gate anything. On GitHub Actions this is \"if: false\" or \"if: ${{ false }}\" on the step or job. On GitLab CI it is \"when: never\", \"when: manual\" (a manual job does not run unless someone starts it, and is allowed to fail by default), rules: that always resolve to never or manual, or a security template disabled with SAST_DISABLED, SECRET_DETECTION_DISABLED, DEPENDENCY_SCANNING_DISABLED, CONTAINER_SCANNING_DISABLED or DAST_DISABLED set to \"true\". Other rules stay quiet for disabled scanners, since the scanner does not run at all."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The scanner is configured but never runs automatically, so it cannot gate anything. On GitHub Actions this is \"if: false\" or \"if: ${{ false }}\" on the step or job. On GitLab CI it is \"when: never\", \"when: manual\" (a manual job does not run unless someone starts it, and is allowed to fail by default), rules: that always resolve to never or manual, or a security template disabled with SAST_DISABLED, SECRET_DETECTION_DISABLED, DEPENDENCY_SCANNING_DISABLED, CONTAINER_SCANNING_DISABLED or DAST_DISABLED set to \"true\". Other rules stay quiet for disabled scanners, since the scanner does not run at all.\n\nFix: remove the condition that disables the scanner",
                "markdown": "The scanner is configured but never runs automatically, so it cannot gate anything. On GitHub Actions this is \"if: false\" or \"if: ${{ false }}\" on the step or job. On GitLab CI it is \"when: never\", \"when: manual\" (a manual job does not run unless someone starts it, and is allowed to fail by default), rules: that always resolve to never or manual, or a security template disabled with SAST_DISABLED, SECRET_DETECTION_DISABLED, DEPENDENCY_SCANNING_DISABLED, CONTAINER_SCANNING_DISABLED or DAST_DISABLED set to \"true\". Other rules stay quiet for disabled scanners, since the scanner does not run at all.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\ninclude:\n  - template: Jobs/Secret-Detection.gitlab-ci.yml\nvariables:\n  SECRET_DETECTION_DISABLED: \"true\"\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\ninclude:\n  - template: Jobs/Secret-Detection.gitlab-ci.yml\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL009.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "8.0"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "GL010",
              "name": "SuppressionWithoutAReason",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Suppression without a reason"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A \"# gatelint:ignore\" comment must name the rule IDs it suppresses and give a reason of at least 10 characters, so reviewers can tell a deliberate exception from a silenced warning. A suppression without a valid reason is reported by this rule and does not suppress anything; a suppression naming an unknown rule ID is reported as well. GL010 findings cannot themselves be suppressed."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "A \"# gatelint:ignore\" comment must name the rule IDs it suppresses and give a reason of at least 10 characters, so reviewers can tell a deliberate exception from a silenced warning. A suppression without a valid reason is reported by this rule and does not suppress anything; a suppression naming an unknown rule ID is reported as well. GL010 findings cannot themselves be suppressed.\n\nFix: add a reason of at least 10 characters after the rule IDs",
                "markdown": "A \"# gatelint:ignore\" comment must name the rule IDs it suppresses and give a reason of at least 10 characters, so reviewers can tell a deliberate exception from a silenced warning. A suppression without a valid reason is reported by this rule and does not suppress anything; a suppression naming an unknown rule ID is reported as well. GL010 findings cannot themselves be suppressed.\n\n**Before**\n\n```yaml\n# gatelint:ignore GL004\ncontinue-on-error: true\n```\n\n**After**\n\n```yaml\n# gatelint:ignore GL004 nightly job only reports to the dashboard; the PR gate is in ci.yml\ncontinue-on-error: true\n```"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/codewithQasimAli/gatelint/blob/main/docs/rules/GL010.md",
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "note"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "ci"
                ],
                "precision": "high",
                "security-severity": "3.0"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "invocations": [
        {
          "executionSuccessful": true,
          "toolExecutionNotifications": [
            {
              "level": "note",
